[[00:10:55.550]]
SPEAKER 11
going on. But let's start by talking about the challenge we're facing with the coronavirus. We do have a couple of health care experts here who can answer more detailed questions as they come up in the town hall, but I just wanted to give you some general sense. We are, by the way, we're Facebook-ing this, if I stay in the right spot, so people can participate without coming to a town hall if they felt uncomfortable doing so. What we're facing, you might think of it a little bit like the equivalent of a scary flu season. And we're in person-to-person contact, the disease moves, and it moves through people coughing or sneezing and you proceed to have it on your hands from a handrail or who knows what. And you put your hand to your nose or your mouth and then you get infected. uh so that's why there's so much emphasis right now on really trying to affect transmission which is why i'm trying to be disciplined you saw i just forgot just now i almost shook hands with jim and he reminded me uh so it really helps to wash your hands to use hand sanitizer, to consciously try not to put your hands to your face, and those things interrupt the infections. We do have several new cases in Oregon that we just announced this morning. We had three cases, now I think we have a total of seven. And some of those are from travel, and one is from person-to-person contact. We'll learn more about them. We are seeing the numbers go up very rapidly, and that's very scary. We had a briefing on Thursday morning with the Secretary Azar and the top health professionals. And at that briefing, they said, "Well, we now have 120 identified cases." Well, here we are on Saturday morning, two days later, we have more than 300 identified cases. If you went back seven days ago, we had 26 identified cases, so 26, 120, 300. Now, a good part of that is that test kits are being now moved throughout the country to try to get test kits everywhere. So we're testing people we weren't testing before. So it doesn't mean this disease is moving that fast, it probably means the test kits are getting distributed and we're testing more people. Most people who are younger with healthy immune systems will see very mild symptoms. It's the disease is the sort of thing that attacks people who have particular health care challenges with the immune system or lung issues or are older and more frail and fragile. So in that sense it's kind of similar to the way the flu is. Now the flu, think about the flu. Since 2010 we've had bad flu seasons and light flu seasons. In a light flu season, 14,000 Americans died from the flu. That's a significant number. In a heavy flu season, more than 60,000 die. We don't know how this will be proportional to that. I asked on Thursday morning, what is our best guess on the lethality of this disease? And one expert gave one answer, and then another expert set up and said, no, no, no, that's not right at all. That doesn't take into account all the people who are infected who have mild symptoms. And he thought that the lethality best guess would be equivalent to flu, or perhaps up to 10 times worse. That's still significant, variation, so we don't know, but the point is we're trying to break the chain of communication or of flu from one person or the coronavirus from one person to another in, as they may be very thoughtful about our hands, it comes down to our hands. So, that's that. There are so many other issues around the nation, I'll just mention a few of them. We have the challenge of investments in the foundations for families to thrive, issues related to

